  "Question": "Solve the system \\(\\begin{cases}x/2+y/3=3\\\\x-y=1\\end{cases}\\). (Write the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), in that order, separated by a comma.)",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4，3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Multiply both sides of the first equation by 6: \\(3x+2y=18\\). Combine it with \\(x-y=1\\). Multiplying the second equation by 2 gives \\(2x-2y=2\\). Adding gives \\(5x=20\\), so \\(x=4\\), \\(y=3\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Substitute using x-y=1\n\nThe second equation has coefficient \\(1\\) on \\(x\\), so write \\(x=y+1\\). Substituting into the first equation leaves just \\(y\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{y+1}{2}+\\frac{y}{3}=3.\n\\]\nMultiply every term by \\(6\\) to clear the denominators:\n\\[\n3(y+1)+2y=18,\n\\qquad 5y+3=18.\n\\]\nThus \\(y=3\\) and \\(x=y+1=4\\). The answer is \\(4,3\\).",
